TALLY_FINAL_MESSAGE={"verdict":"blocked","diagnosis":"Reproduced the cp-proxy failure deterministically as a fork-API contract bug in scripts/make-proxy.sh, not a checkpoint, machinery, or environment fault. Evidence and mechanism:\n- The traceback's \"<stdin>\" is the container heredoc that begins at scripts/make-proxy.sh:127, so embedded-body line 239 is scripts/make-proxy.sh:365 (`for name, entry in header.items():`) and body line 406 is the `main()` call at the end of that heredoc.\n- `header` comes from `read_header(path, parse)` at scripts/make-proxy.sh:310-316, which does `return parse(path)` unchanged when the fork exposes `parse_safetensors_header` (bound at scripts/make-proxy.sh:351 via `getattr(ple_mmap, \"parse_safetensors_header\", None)`).\n- The fork's function is typed `def parse_safetensors_header(path: str) -> tuple[dict, int]` and returns `header, data_start` (confirmed by reading /opt/vllm/vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py inside flashnext:dev). So `header` is a 2-tuple and `.items()` raises AttributeError: 'tuple' object has no attribute 'items'.\n- The struct-unpack fallback at scripts/make-proxy.sh:314-316 returns a plain dict, so the defect only fires when the fork module actually imports, i.e. only inside the container at checkpoint time. proxy-tooling's acceptance is grep/compile-only and its unit tests never drive `read_header`, which is why authoring passed and cp-proxy is the first place it surfaces.\n- Host-side reproduction with no GPU and no container: extract the largest heredoc from scripts/make-proxy.sh, import it the way tests/test_proxy_tooling.py does, and call `read_header(\"/dev/null\", lambda p: ({\"t\": {\"dtype\": \"F32\", \"shape\": [1]}}, 8))`; it returns a tuple instead of a mapping.\n- The fix is one line: unwrap the pair, e.g. `header, _data_start = parse(path)` then `return header`. The fork already pops `__metadata__`, so the existing `if name == \"__metadata__\"` guard downstream stays harmless and no other call site changes.\n- Nothing downstream of that point has executed yet, so there is no evidence for or against run-proxy.sh serve-time behavior; make-proxy aborts before writing any file.\nWhy blocked rather than retry: cp-proxy is kind=checkpoint, its brief states \"Do not modify the repository\", its argv is fixed by the worklist, and scripts/make-proxy.sh sits in the proxy-tooling task's conflictDomains, so the corrective edit is wholly outside this task's authority; checkpoint tasks never retry, and re-running the same fixed argv against the same base would fail identically.","proposal":{"kind":"amendment-task","paths":["scripts/make-proxy.sh","tests/test_proxy_tooling.py"],"goal":"Amend proxy-tooling (rev 3) to fix the fork-API contract bug that aborts scripts/make-proxy.sh inside the container, and to pin the contract with a test that runs on the host. Change 1: in the embedded builder body of scripts/make-proxy.sh, `read_header(path, parse)` must return only the header mapping. The fork's `parse_safetensors_header` is typed `-> tuple[dict, int]` and returns `(header, data_start)`, so `return parse(path)` hands `main()` a tuple and `header.items()` raises AttributeError: 'tuple' object has no attribute 'items'. Unwrap it, e.g. `header, _data_start = parse(path)` followed by `return header`, keeping the struct-unpack fallback (which already returns a dict) byte-compatible with the fork path. The fork pops `__metadata__` itself, so leave the existing `__metadata__` guard in main() in place; the offset half is genuinely unused by this generator, so discard it rather than threading it through. Change 2: add a unit test to tests/test_proxy_tooling.py that imports the extracted builder body via the existing `_builder_module()` helper and asserts `read_header` returns a mapping when the injected `parse` callable returns the fork's `(header, data_start)` pair, so a future re-introduction of the tuple leak fails on the host instead of at checkpoint time on the GPU. Do not widen scope: no serve run, no GPU work, no change to run-proxy.sh flags or to results/receipts/proxy.json content, and no edits outside the two listed paths. Honour the standing overseer notes: land one non-empty commit on the assigned branch inside these ownership paths, and keep the scripts idempotent and side-effect-free on tracked files. After this lands, cp-proxy can be re-dispatched unchanged.","acceptanceCriteria":[{"id":"read-header-returns-a-mapping","description":"The builder's read_header unwraps the fork's (header, data_start) pair and returns the header mapping; this reproduces the cp-proxy AttributeError today and must pass after the fix.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport importlib.util, pathlib, re, tempfile\nbody = max(re.findall(r\"<<'PY'[^\\n]*\\n(.*?)\\nPY\\n\", pathlib.Path('scripts/make-proxy.sh').read_text(), re.S), key=len)\ntmp = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())/'b.py'; tmp.write_text(body)\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('b', tmp); m = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(m)\nh = m.read_header('/dev/null', lambda p: ({'t': {'dtype': 'F32', 'shape': [1]}}, 8))\nassert hasattr(h, 'items'), 'read_header did not unwrap the fork tuple'\nassert list(h) == ['t'], h\nEOF"]},{"id":"proxy-tooling-unit-tests-pass","description":"The proxy tooling unit suite, including the new read_header contract test, passes on the host with no GPU and no container.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_proxy_tooling.py' -q"]},{"id":"proxy-scripts-still-clean","description":"Both scripts remain syntax-clean, every embedded Python body still compiles, and run-proxy.sh still references the receipt path.","argv":["bash","-euc","bash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh scripts/run-proxy.sh && grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh"]},{"id":"proxy-lane-clean","description":"The lane is fully committed: no uncommitted or untracked files remain after the amendment commit.","argv":["bash","-euc","test -z \"$(git status --porcelain)\""]}],"dependencies":["container-recipe"]}}
